WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.360
170,000 U.S. military personnel&nbsp;
are deployed overseas.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:13.440
Of them, about sixty-thousand, or 35%, are&nbsp;
stationed in Japan — more than any other country.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.720
In fact, only two — Germany and&nbsp;
South Korea — even come close.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.360
And while this number has been&nbsp;
slowly declining in most places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.720
troop deployments in Japan&nbsp;
are actually on the rise.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.880
Its islands are home to over 80 American bases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.840
and the Navy’s only forward-deployed&nbsp;
aircraft carrier, USS Ronald Reagan.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.640
The U.S.-Japan alliance is among&nbsp;
the strongest in the world.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.200
In the last century, Japanese&nbsp;
leaders have made more State,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.680
Official, and Official Working visits&nbsp;
to the U.S. than any other ally.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.680
The public in both countries also overwhelmingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.760
view the other favorably — 71%&nbsp;
of Japanese and 87% of Americans.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.040
Yet there has always been a&nbsp;
fundamental sense of imbalance.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.720
The United States is, unquestionably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:15.440
Japan’s closest ally. But Japan is&nbsp;
only one of many close American allies.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.920
One needs the other for its continued existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.360
whereas the other only wants&nbsp;
the benefit of cooperation.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.640
For being friends, the two countries exchange&nbsp;
a whole lot of money, leading many to wonder:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.880
“Who gets the better deal?”

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.480
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.320
Nebula — where you can watch this bonus video&nbsp;
about Japan’s almost-nuclear deterrence.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.080
The relationship between the U.S.&nbsp;
and Japan has never been “normal”.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.960
Unlike NATO allies, who are&nbsp;
committed to defending one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.840
theirs is one-sided. While the&nbsp;
U.S. is bound to protect Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.680
which it accomplishes through extended&nbsp;
nuclear deterrence, the reverse is not true.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:10.320
Instead, the arrangement began as a strategic&nbsp;
trade — security in exchange for bases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.760
which gave the American military&nbsp;
a front-row seat in Asia.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.360
It was agreed that Japan would provide&nbsp;
all the facilities, airfields, and ports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.840
while the U.S. would pay for everything else.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.160
At the time, all this seemed perfectly fair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.920
Japan, after all, was then&nbsp;
completely devastated by the war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.840
In fact, it could very easily be argued&nbsp;
that, as the victor, the U.S. took advantage.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:44.720
As time went on, however, Japan only got richer&nbsp;
and richer. So too did the Yen gain in value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.480
The circumstances had changed&nbsp;
dramatically, yet the deal had not.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.440
And so, under pressure from the Americans, in 1978&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.080
Japan began making payments above and&nbsp;
beyond what was required by the treaty.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.680
Because these payments were designed to&nbsp;
demonstrate understanding for the costs — monetary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.640
and otherwise — shouldered by the Americans,&nbsp;
they became known as the “Sympathy Budget”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.320
The U.S. prefers the term “Host-Nation Support”.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.360
While making clear this was completely voluntary,&nbsp;
Japan built new facilities, paid their utilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.160
and covered the salaries of its&nbsp;
citizens who worked in them.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.720
Then two things happened which&nbsp;
raised concerns of loyalty.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:35.200
First, Japan refused to help secure the&nbsp;
Persian Gulf during the Iran-Iraq war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.560
despite being explicitly&nbsp;
asked to do so by the U.S.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:46.320
And second, Toshiba was caught around the same&nbsp;
time selling military technology to the Soviets.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:54.080
The country was at serious risk of being seen&nbsp;
as a bad ally. It knew it had to do something.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.800
That something turned out to be formalizing&nbsp;
the previously-voluntary annual payments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:07.200
into contracts called Special Measure&nbsp;
Agreements, to be renegotiated every 5 years.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.840
Japan’s contributions climbed&nbsp;
immediately to a new high.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:20.080
In the 40 years between 1978 and 2016,&nbsp;
Japan paid something like $65 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.360
in total for U.S. forces, with an&nbsp;
average increase of 17% a year.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:33.680
In 2019, it contributed roughly $3.16 billion&nbsp;
to the U.S. military when everything from labor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.360
to utilities, the cost to relocate training&nbsp;
exercises away from civilian populations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.840
and facility upgrades are included.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.160
But what do these numbers&nbsp;
really mean, you may wonder.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:53.440
In 2017, Japanese politicians claimed the&nbsp;
country shoulders 86% of the total cost to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:02.000
station U.S. troops there. The Pentagon came up&nbsp;
with 74% in 2004. Other sources go as low as 50%.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:09.840
Whatever the true figure, it’s higher than&nbsp;
South Korea — around 40% — or Germany — 32.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.960
None of these numbers, however, are complete.&nbsp;
Many contributions simply can’t be quantified.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.240
These include things like indirect subsidies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.760
the infrastructure around bases,&nbsp;
like roads, waived taxes and fees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.360
and, quite substantially, the rents that could’ve&nbsp;
been collected from the land occupied by bases.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:37.840
Last but not least, Japan buys nearly&nbsp;
all its defense equipment from the U.S.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.080
The biggest sacrifice made&nbsp;
by the Japanese government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:45.040
however, is not monetary, but political.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.600
Politicians are caught between&nbsp;
two very powerful forces.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:55.200
On one side is the public, who&nbsp;
overwhelmingly opposes militarization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:01.200
Anything resembling an expansion of the&nbsp;
military will be met with intense scrutiny.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.400
No party, no matter how popular, can&nbsp;
afford to ignore the will of voters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.480
on this issue — which means very carefully&nbsp;
tip-toeing around the entire subject of defense.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.400
In 2015, for instance, Shinzo Abe&nbsp;
passed a security law allowing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:24.240
military to protect its allies under certain&nbsp;
circumstances, in addition to self-defense — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.880
right so basic and universal that it’s&nbsp;
enshrined in the United Nations charter.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.720
For this, he paid dearly — as you can&nbsp;
clearly see in this graph of approval rating.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.160
On one day alone, 300 protests were&nbsp;
staged across the country — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:47.200
largest of which attracted 120,000 people.
Shinzo Abe’s relative success in politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:53.280
came despite his perceived “hawkishness”, not&nbsp;
because of it. In the run-up to elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:59.120
he would cleverly shift focus to the economy,&nbsp;
only turning to defense after he had won.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.280
On the other side is the United States.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.880
In America, where no equivalent “peace&nbsp;
culture” exists, Japan’s unwillingness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.240
to expand the size and role of its military&nbsp;
is not some noble commitment to Pacifism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.600
but somewhere between greedy and dangerous.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.360
Many would like for it to take on a greater share&nbsp;
of the financial burden for the sake of fairness.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.520
Others, including much of the&nbsp;
foreign policy establishment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:32.880
see Japan as relying too heavily on the&nbsp;
U.S., at the expense of its own military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:36.560
and ultimately, the security of&nbsp;
the entire Asia-Pacific region.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:43.520
Thus, Japanese politicians must carefully&nbsp;
balance these two forces — in the short-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:50.240
satisfying the demands of voters or else losing&nbsp;
power, while simultaneously meeting the rising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:55.360
long-term expectations of its ally, or&nbsp;
else losing its sole source of protection.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:59.440
This tension came to a head&nbsp;
during the First Gulf War.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:05.840
In December 1990, the U.S. Ambassador called&nbsp;
the Japanese Prime Minister on the phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.400
asking a longstanding ally for support&nbsp;
against the Iraqi invasion of Kuwait.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:17.600
But doing so was deemed unconstitutional. In one&nbsp;
poll, just one third of respondents supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:23.120
sending forces, though with one (hilarious)&nbsp;
condition: that they would not be armed.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.840
In the end, Japan refused.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:33.280
Only after a ceasefire was announced, did it&nbsp;
send 6 small, vintage wooden minesweeping ships.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.560
Even this was controversial back home.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.160
The international community was furious.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:48.240
Instead of sending forces, Japan contributed&nbsp;
cash — $13 billion, or 20% of the total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.800
cost — though it stipulated the money could&nbsp;
only be used for “non-lethal” purposes.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:58.800
Yet, with zero casualties to the U.S.’&nbsp;
over one hundred, it didn’t sit right.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.880
Japan was accused of “checkbook diplomacy” —&nbsp;
paying money to dodge its obligations rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:07.840
than doing the “noble” thing&nbsp;
and putting lives on the line.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:13.440
In truth, its assistance was far from&nbsp;
necessary — the victory came easy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:17.440
It wasn’t about money or&nbsp;
need. It was about fairness.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.080
Japanese politicians chose to&nbsp;
obey the will of voters, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:27.280
opposed the war, but, in doing so, lost a&nbsp;
great deal of international credibility.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:29.760
But the lesson stuck.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:36.800
Ten years later, on September 11th, 2001, it took&nbsp;
the Japanese government just forty-five minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:41.360
after the attack to set up a task force&nbsp;
and declare its full support for the U.S.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.000
Within a month, Japan had passed&nbsp;
an unprecedented law that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.960
later allow boots on the ground in Iraq,&nbsp;
despite popular opposition and protests.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:57.520
Since then, its troops have been sent to Cambodia,&nbsp;
Mozambique, Rwanda, and a dozen other countries.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:00.880
But this is not quite what it seems.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:06.080
The government only managed to sell&nbsp;
this dramatic shift in defense policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:11.360
at least by Japanese standards, by imposing&nbsp;
insanely strict restrictions on itself.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:15.920
The law only permits deployments&nbsp;
of up to 2,000 troops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.280
only for UN peacekeeping operations, and&nbsp;
only if five specific conditions are met.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.800
The Japanese Self Defense Forces are&nbsp;
forbidden from infantry tasks, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:32.400
doing things like collecting weapons, and can&nbsp;
only operate in areas explicitly deemed “safe”.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:38.160
Japanese troops often require dedicated&nbsp;
protection from troops of other nations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.280
which is sometimes derisively&nbsp;
labeled “babysitting”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:47.200
They arrive only after bullets have stopped&nbsp;
flying, leave at the earliest sign of trouble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:51.600
and because they’re forbidden from protecting&nbsp;
allies, are sometimes more of a liability.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.520
None has ever died on a mission overseas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:59.840
and a single death would instantly&nbsp;
trigger a crisis back home.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:04.480
This solution, in other words, narrowly satisfies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.920
both parties — the public and&nbsp;
the U.S., but only marginally.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:16.160
The trouble is that the two opposing forces&nbsp;
continue growing further apart over time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:22.720
Contrary to expectations, young Japanese,&nbsp;
even without a visceral memory of the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.920
have not waned in their cultural&nbsp;
inclination towards peace.

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:28.160
Yet the American public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:33.040
and by extension, decision-makers,&nbsp;
increasingly demand more from its allies.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.720
And while accusations of unfairness reached&nbsp;
a fever pitch during the Trump presidency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:40.480
they are by no means new.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:48.400
Whoever the president and whatever the global&nbsp;
circumstances, Japan faces the same basic dilemma:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:53.920
Public opposition prevents it from ever&nbsp;
taking control of its own defense destiny.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.360
Yet, only one country can fill&nbsp;
this hole: the United States.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:08.080
In short, Japan has one option, and one&nbsp;
option only. Who wouldn’t be anxious?

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:14.960
If it were ever threatened with war, it would have&nbsp;
no choice but to hope for protection by the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:22.080
That is, except for one, very last&nbsp;
resort — the nuclear option, if you will.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:28.320
In this bonus video, I explain how&nbsp;
Japan could acquire nukes — in a pinch.

